# Totally nonnegative left production matrices

Let $Q=(q_{n,k})_{n,k\geq0}$ and let the lower-triangular array
$A=(a_{n,k})_{n,k\geq0}$ be generated by
\[
a_{n,0}=q_{n,0},\qquad
a_{n+1,k+1}=\sum_{j=k}^{n}q_{n+1,j+1}a_{j,k}.
\]
This is the entrywise form of the left-production factorization
$A=Q\,\operatorname{diag}(I_1,Q)\operatorname{diag}(I_2,Q)\cdots$.

Suppose $Q$ is lower triangular and every finite leading principal truncation of $Q$
is totally nonnegative. Then every row $(a_{n,k})_{k\geq0}$ is a Pólya-frequency
sequence. Consequently, its generating polynomial
$\sum_{k=0}^{n}a_{n,k}x^k$ splits over $\mathbb R$ and all its roots are nonpositive.

The matrix step formalizes Chen--Fu--Ruan's Theorem 1.4: the transpose of every finite
leading Toeplitz matrix of row $n$ is a submatrix of a product of shifted block copies
of the leading truncation $Q_n$. Total nonnegativity is preserved by these identity-block
extensions, finite products, submatrices, and transpose. The Pólya-frequency conclusion
is their Theorem 1.3(3), completed with the Aissen--Schoenberg--Whitney theorem.

## References

- X. Chen, L. Fu, and J. Ruan,
  [*A unified planar network approach to total positivity of combinatorial matrices and
  real-rootedness of polynomials*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.08369), Theorems 1.3 and 1.4.
